<td id="S5.T5.3.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.0</td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">92.6</td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T5.3.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">80.9</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.3.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">   + Subsampling</th>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">41.9</td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.1</td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">92.5</td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">80.8</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.3.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.3.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">   + Padding</th>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.3.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">43.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.3.1.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">36.9</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.3.1.7.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">93.0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.3.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">79.7</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.T5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation on DeBias-CLIP components<span id="S5.T5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. Best performing methods are </span>bolded<span id="S5.T5.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. Training with a caption that excludes the summary sentence improves long retrieval (Urban1k and DOCCI). Adding sentence sampling and padding boosts short-caption retrieval (COCO and Flickr) while preserving long-caption gains.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.22419v1/lambda_effects.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:316/190;" width="316" height="190"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Top-1 text-to-image retrieval on COCO (short) and DOCCI (long) as a function of short caption loss weight <math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.<span id="S5.F5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Short retrieval peaks at <math id="S5.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{s}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{s}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, while long retrieval peaks at <math id="S5.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{s}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{s}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math>. Higher values degrade performance in both cases.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.4.2 </span>Short-Caption Loss Coefficient</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We vary the short-caption loss coefficient from (<a href="#S4.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 4.5 Multi-caption Training ‣ 4 Method ‣ CLIP Is Shortsighted: Paying Attention Beyond the First Sentence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) from 0 to 1, and compare performance on COCO and DOCCI in <a href="#S5.F5" title="In 5.4.1 Contribution of Caption-Level Augmentations ‣ 5.4 Ablations ‣ 5 Experiments and Results ‣ CLIP Is Shortsighted: Paying Attention Beyond the First Sentence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We find that a small weight of <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lambda^{s}=0.1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lambda^{s}=0.1}</annotation></semantics></math> provides the best tradeoff between short and long retrieval, while above <math id="S5.SS4.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lambda^{s}=0.25}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lambda^{s}=0.25}</annotation></semantics></math>, performance degrades for both cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion and Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we empirically show that CLIP-style text encoders exhibit early-token and summary-sentence biases that hinder long-text retrieval. Standard training approaches maintain these biases because the first sentence in training captions is typically a global summary of the entire long caption. To counter this, we propose <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DeBias-CLIP</em>, which trains without the first summary sentence, uses sentence sampling, and adds text token padding. Our method delivers state-of-the-art long-text retrieval while maintaining short-text performance and improving robustness to sentence permutations and summary sentence removal. However, many long-text benchmarks share the same “summary-first” caption structure, which can mask position bias. Our permutation tests reveal this issue and underscore the need for new evaluation benchmarks that more accurately reflect real-world long-context retrieval, beyond caption datasets (e.g., matching images to multi-paragraph document pages, such as textbooks and reports).</p>
</div>
</section>
