<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">37.2</td>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">94.1</td>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">81.3</td></tr>
<tr id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">10</th>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">43.1</td>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">37.0</td>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">94.2</td>
<td id="Ax1.T8.3.1.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">81.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="Ax1.T8.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 8</span>: </span><span id="Ax1.T8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation on the number of training epochs for text-to-image (T2I) retrieval<span id="Ax1.T8.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. We observe significant improvements when going from 1 to 3 epochs across all datasets. Extending training from 5 to 7 epochs yields smaller but still notable improvements on long retrieval (Urban1k and DOCCI). Results from 7 to 10 epochs are more marginal and mixed.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">C.3 </span>Ablation on Sentence Sampling Method</h4>

<div id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our DeBias-CLIP method generates short captions by first generating a random number of sentences <math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n_{\mathrm{sampled}}=\mathcal{U}\left\{1\,,2\,,\dots,\,n_{\mathrm{sents}}-1\right\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>sampled</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>sents</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n_{\mathrm{sampled}}=\mathcal{U}\left\{1\,,2\,,\dots,\,n_{\mathrm{sents}}-1\right\}}</annotation></semantics></math> from a uniform distribution, and then sampling that number without replacement from the original set of sentences <math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mathrm{no\_sum}}=[s_{2},\,\dots,\,s_{n_{\mathrm{sents}}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>no</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sum</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>sents</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mathrm{no\_sum}}=[s_{2},\,\dots,\,s_{n_{\mathrm{sents}}}]</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., with the first sentence excluded), and finally concatenating the sentences together (<em id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Random</em> method). In this section, we consider four alternative sampling methods:</p>
<ul id="Ax1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="Ax1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Ax1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ordered</em>: We select a contiguous block of sentences starting from the second sentence up to a randomly selected end sentence, preserving the original narrative order.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Independent</em>: We independently select each sentence in <math id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\mathrm{no\_sum}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>no</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sum</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\mathrm{no\_sum}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a probability <math id="Ax1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, creating a more uniform number of sentences.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Keep 4</em>: We fix the sample size to <math id="Ax1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\mathrm{sampled}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>sampled</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\mathrm{sampled}}=4</annotation></semantics></math> while sampling randomly without replacement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Ax1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Ax1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Ax1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Shuffle</em>: We use the full set of sentences (no subsampling) but randomize their order.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Results are presented in <a href="#Ax1.T9" title="In C.3 Ablation on Sentence Sampling Method ‣ C Additional Ablations ‣ Appendices ‣ CLIP Is Shortsighted: Paying Attention Beyond the First Sentence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tab.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. We find that performance differences between most sampling methods are marginal. Sampling contiguous sentences (<em id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ordered</em>) improves short-text retrieval (<math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Flickr) at the expense of long-text retrieval (<math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Urban1k). This is similar to the SmartCLIP strategy, differing only in that SmartCLIP includes the initial summary sentence (<math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>). Conversely, using all sentences (<em id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Shuffle</em>) boosts long retrieval (<math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> on DOCCI) but causes significant degradation in short retrieval (<math id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.2\%</annotation></semantics></math> on Flickr). Ultimately, we find that our default <em id="Ax1.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Random</em> sampling offers the best balance between short and long retrieval without requiring additional parameter tuning.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Ax1.T9" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Ax1.T9.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
